<p data-start=3D"3609" data-end=3D"3627" class=3D"">Dans cet exemple :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"3628" data-end=3D"4015">
<li data-start=3D"3628" data-end=3D"3715" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"3630" data-end=3D"3715" class=3D"">Nous avons d=C3=A9fini =
une <strong data-start=3D"3652" data-end=3D"3674">interface <code data-star=
t=3D"3664" data-end=3D"3672">Animal</code></strong> avec deux m=C3=A9thodes=
 <code data-start=3D"3694" data-end=3D"3702">manger</code> et <code data-st=
art=3D"3706" data-end=3D"3714">dormir</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3716" data-end=3D"3837" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"3718" data-end=3D"3837" class=3D"">La classe <strong data-=
start=3D"3728" data-end=3D"3737">Chien</strong> impl=C3=A9mente cette inter=
face, et elle doit fournir des <strong data-start=3D"3791" data-end=3D"3820=
">impl=C3=A9mentations concr=C3=A8tes</strong> de ces m=C3=A9thodes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3838" data-end=3D"4015" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"3840" data-end=3D"4015" class=3D"">Dans la m=C3=A9thode <c=
ode data-start=3D"3856" data-end=3D"3862">main</code>, nous avons cr=C3=A9=
=C3=A9 un objet de type <code data-start=3D"3897" data-end=3D"3905">Animal<=
/code> (qui fait r=C3=A9f=C3=A9rence =C3=A0 un objet de type <code data-sta=
rt=3D"3945" data-end=3D"3952">Chien</code>), et nous avons appel=C3=A9 les =
m=C3=A9thodes d=C3=A9finies dans l'interface.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start=3D"4017" data-end=3D"4050" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
4021" data-end=3D"4050">3.2 Remarques Importantes</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"4051" data-end=3D"4338">
<li data-start=3D"4051" data-end=3D"4212" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4053" data-end=3D"4212" class=3D"">Si une classe impl=C3=
=A9mente une interface et ne fournit pas d'impl=C3=A9mentation pour toutes =
les m=C3=A9thodes de l'interface, elle doit =C3=AAtre d=C3=A9clar=C3=A9e co=
mme <strong data-start=3D"4198" data-end=3D"4211">abstraite</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4213" data-end=3D"4338" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4215" data-end=3D"4338" class=3D"">Une interface peut =C3=
=AAtre <strong data-start=3D"4239" data-end=3D"4276">impl=C3=A9ment=C3=A9e =
par plusieurs classes</strong>, et une classe peut impl=C3=A9menter plusieu=
rs interfaces en Java.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"4340" data-end=3D"4343" class=3D"">
<h2 data-start=3D"4345" data-end=3D"4382" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
4348" data-end=3D"4382">4. Interfaces et Polymorphisme</strong></h2>
<p data-start=3D"4384" data-end=3D"4647" class=3D"">Les interfaces jouent u=
n r=C3=B4le cl=C3=A9 dans le <strong data-start=3D"4426" data-end=3D"4443">=
polymorphisme</strong>. Un objet de type <strong data-start=3D"4462" data-e=
nd=3D"4475">interface</strong> peut =C3=AAtre utilis=C3=A9 pour r=C3=A9f=C3=
=A9rencer des objets de diff=C3=A9rentes classes qui impl=C3=A9mentent cett=
e interface. Cela permet de traiter diff=C3=A9rents types d'objets de mani=
=C3=A8re uniforme.</p>
