<h2 data-start=3D"659" data-end=3D"721" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"66=
2" data-end=3D"719">1. D=C3=A9finition du Dependency Inversion Principle (D=
IP)</strong></h2>
<p data-start=3D"723" data-end=3D"778" class=3D"">Le <strong data-start=3D"=
726" data-end=3D"733">DIP</strong> repose sur <strong data-start=3D"745" da=
ta-end=3D"774">deux r=C3=A8gles fondamentales</strong> :</p>
<ol data-start=3D"780" data-end=3D"1123">
<li data-start=3D"780" data-end=3D"958" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"783" data-end=3D"866" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"783=
" data-end=3D"864">Les modules de haut niveau ne doivent pas d=C3=A9pendre =
des modules de bas niveau.</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"870" data-end=3D"958">
<li data-start=3D"870" data-end=3D"954" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"872" data-end=3D"954" class=3D"">Les deux doivent d=C3=A9p=
endre d=E2=80=99<strong data-start=3D"900" data-end=3D"916">abstractions</s=
trong> (interfaces ou classes abstraites).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"959" data-end=3D"1123" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"962" data-end=3D"1021" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"96=
2" data-end=3D"1019">Les abstractions ne doivent pas d=C3=A9pendre des d=C3=
=A9tails.</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"1025" data-end=3D"1123">
<li data-start=3D"1025" data-end=3D"1123" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1027" data-end=3D"1123" class=3D"">Ce sont les <strong dat=
a-start=3D"1039" data-end=3D"1050">d=C3=A9tails</strong> (impl=C3=A9mentati=
ons concr=C3=A8tes) qui doivent d=C3=A9pendre des <strong data-start=3D"110=
4" data-end=3D"1120">abstractions</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"1125" data-end=3D"1332" class=3D"">En d'autres termes, <st=
rong data-start=3D"1145" data-end=3D"1187">les d=C3=A9pendances doivent =C3=
=AAtre invers=C3=A9es</strong> : au lieu de coder une classe qui d=C3=A9pen=
d directement d=E2=80=99une autre classe concr=C3=A8te, nous utilisons une =
<strong data-start=3D"1289" data-end=3D"1304">abstraction</strong> pour d=
=C3=A9coupler ces modules.</p>
